<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Ceylon</h1><p class="st-meta">1597 CE – 1658 CE · capital Colombo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Portuguese colony on the coasts of Sri Lanka, created by the bequest of the last Kotte king and lost to the Dutch.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Portuguese had held a fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> since the early 16th century and dominated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kotte">Kingdom of Kotte</a>. When its Christian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmapala-of-kotte">Dharmapala</a> died in 1597, the Portuguese Crown claimed his realm under his will, making the Sinhalese lowlands a colony; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> was added in 1619.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was administered by a captain-general at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-india">Portuguese India</a>. The Portuguese drew revenue from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>, collected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salagama">Salagama</a> peelers as a service obligation, and from areca and elephants. Catholic missions converted many coastal people, especially fishing communities, and Portuguese words entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinhala-language">Sinhala</a> and Tamil. Repeated campaigns against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a> failed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasinha-ii-of-kandy">Rajasinha II of Kandy</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> in 1640, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> in 1656 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> in 1658, ending Portuguese rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> kept the coast, creating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-ceylon">Dutch Ceylon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-ceylon.jpg" alt="Portuguese Ceylon" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: myself, based on ancient national symbol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(1640).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1597 CE – 1658 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Colombo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Constantino de Sa de Noronha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Sinhala, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ceilao, Portuguese Sri Lanka</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1628&amp;focus=portuguese-ceylon" aria-label="Portuguese Ceylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6891 1919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Ceylon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7211 2175L7023 1863M7211 2175L7212 2176M7211 2175L7211 2175M7211 2175L7208 2073"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7023 1863h0M7212 2176h0M7211 2175h0M7208 2073h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7211 2175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7211 2175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Colombo. <a href="/atlas?year=1628&amp;focus=portuguese-ceylon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
